    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "SPIRITS\nKentucky Bourbon • Canadian Whiskey\nGin • Light \u0026 Gold Rum • Scotch Whiskey\nTennessee Whiskey • Vodka\nRed-headed Woodpecker\nNoisy and quarrelsome, these\nhandsome birds are the only eastern\nwoodpeckers with completely red\nheads. Their four toes-two in front\nand two in back-identify them as\ntree-climbing birds; their stiff tail\nfeathers are used to help prop\nthem against tree trunks.\nRanges from Canada south\nto Florida and the Gulf Coast\nand west to New Mexico,\nColorado and eastern\nWyoming.\nRivoli's Hummingbird\nNo other bird flies like the\nhummingbird flies! Naturés most amazing\naerialists, these exquisite creatures can eat, drink, fight,\ncourt, and mate while suspended in flight. Their\nextraordinary wings are powered by the largest chest\nmuscles for their size of any animal. Rivoli's\nHummingbird can be found in the mountains of\nsoutheast Arizona, southwestern New Mexico, and\nwestern Texas.\nCOCKTAILS AND APERITIS\nManhattan • Whiskey Sour • Screwdriver\nBloody Mary • Vodka Martini • Gin Martini\nGin and Tonic • Dry Sack Sherry\nCORDIALS\nCourvoisier V.S. Cognac • Creme de Menthe\nKahlua • Drambuie • Grand Marnier\nAmaretto di Saronno\nAmerican Beer - Tomato Juice - Orange luce • perrer. CocaCola. TaB E. i. E\nEuropean Tree Sparrow\nAlthough introduced\ninto the United States at\nSt. Louis more than one\nhundred wears ago, the\nEuropean Tree Sparrow has\nremained close to that city and\nits surrounding farmland.\nApparently the indigenous bird\npopulation has resisted any\nencroachment upon their\nterritory by these\ngregarious newcomers.\nPrinted in U.S.A.\nF-440-OR-1-9/80\nPrepared with the assistance of the American Museum of Natural History.\nDavid Ploude"
